AB  - Evaluating the performance of decision-making units (DMUs) and forecasting their future performance necessitates the integration of multiple analytical models, especially when dealing with integer inputs, undesirable outputs, and time-series data. This study proposes a hybrid approach that combines a dynamic directional distance function model with a long short-term memory deep learning model to meet these requirements. The directional distance function model accommodates integer inputs and undesirable outputs under both managerial and natural disposability, generating an efficiency index that serves as a novel feature for training the long short-term memory model. The hybrid model aims to minimize prediction errors, as assessed by the root mean squared error and the mean absolute percentage error, to forecast the performance of DMUs accurately. Applied to a dataset from a major Iranian financial institution spanning April 2022 to May 2024, the model demonstrates a low root mean squared error with a two-layer long short-term memory architecture (128 and 64 neurons), indicating reliable performance predictions. Compared to standalone data envelopment analysis (DEA), autoregressive integrated moving average, and recurrent neural network models, the proposed approach achieves a 12.5 % reduction in root mean squared error, enhancing forecasting accuracy for financial institutions.
