AB  - Unexpected building collapses during fire rescue operations seriously threaten firefighters, highlighting the need for reliable, real-time collapse prediction. This study presents a framework for real-time assessment of collapse risk in fire-affected portal frame buildings (REACT-NET) using long short-term memory networks, which utilizes on-site fire and structural response data, along with the building geometry, to forecast displacement evolution at critical locations within a burning structure. These predictions enable real-time evaluation of collapse risk based on predefined displacement-based criteria. REACT-NET was demonstrated on steel portal frame buildings subjected to fire under two measurement plans, with 540 samples generated through finite element simulations. The trained agents achieved acceptable prediction accuracies exceeding 80 %, 90 %, and 95 % for vertical span displacements, horizontal column displacements, and vertical column displacements, respectively, on numerical samples. REACT-Net demonstrated robustness when applied to complex fire and structural conditions not encountered during training. Validation through a real fire test on a scaled portal frame confirmed the feasibility of REACT-Net for real-time collapse prediction, reducing the prediction error of the remaining safe time to collapse to 12 s at 241 s of fire exposure, with a 10 % accuracy improvement compared with the traditional three-level early-warning method. These results highlight the potential of REACT-Net as a practical tool for enhancing firefighter safety and optimizing firefighting strategies.
