AB  - High-resolution local flow measurements were conducted in a vertical six-by-six rod bundle using a custom-designed Pitot tube, and key parameters including instantaneous velocity, mean velocity and vortex frequency were obtained. The mean relative error of the Pitot tube velocity measurements was below 10 percent at a mean liquid phase velocity of 1.087 m per second when compared with high-speed camera measurements, and 5.95 percent across the liquid phase velocity range from 0.389 m per second to 1.087 m per second relative to magnetic flow meter results. On the basis of these experimental data, a data driven approach employing radial basis function neural networks was developed to construct a mixing length turbulence model suitable for complex 6 × 6 rod bundle geometries. The radial basis function regression model was first trained using circular pipe data to establish the relationship between mixing length and local velocity. The model was then extended to the six-by-six rod bundle by introducing a dimensionless correction factor to account for channel box viscosity effects and to improve local velocity predictions. The resulting velocity distribution showed excellent agreement with experimental measurements, with relative errors below 9.61 percent relative to Pitot tube data and below 3.25 percent relative to magnetic flow meter averages. Furthermore, by combining measured local velocities with the developed mixing length model, vortex frequencies from 10 to 28 Hz were predicted reliably. These results demonstrate the proposed data-driven turbulence model is both accurate and practical for predicting flow in rod bundle channels in nuclear systems.
