AB  - —Accurate prediction of sulfur dioxide (SO2) concentration at the outlet of wet flue gas desulfurization (WFGD) systems is crucial for optimizing process control and reducing emissions. However, strong fluctuations and sudden mutations in SO2 concentration make reliable prediction highly challenging. To address this issue, this study proposes a time–frequency dual-branch dynamic fusion network (TFDNET) that integrates temporal and frequency-domain representations to enhance feature learning across multiple scales. The framework enables adaptive information fusion, improving the model's responsiveness to transient variations while maintaining long-term stability. Experiments using real industrial desulfurization data from a zinc smelter show that TFDNET achieves an average reduction of 70.87 % in MSE and 50.52 % in MAE compared with existing models. Additional validation on public benchmark datasets confirms its strong generalization and robustness, demonstrating its potential for intelligent monitoring and control in complex industrial processes.
