AB  - Traditional computational approaches for vibroacoustic analysis of advanced composite structures are prohibitively time-intensive for comprehensive design optimization. This study presents an integrated computational framework combining analytical modeling and deep learning to investigate sound transmission loss (STL) in sandwich cylindrical shells with functionally graded carbon nanotube-reinforced composite (FG-CNTRC) facesheets and hexagonal honeycomb cores. The analytical component employs third-order shear deformation theory (TSDT) and incorporates fluid-structure interaction under subsonic external flow conditions, while the honeycomb core is represented as an orthotropic material through modified Gibson's equations. Material properties of FG-CNTRC facesheets are determined via extended rule of mixtures across four distinct CNT distribution patterns. Vibroacoustic equations are solved through Fourier series expansions to establish baseline STL characteristics. The deep neural network (DNN) component features a carefully optimized architecture comprising eight dense layers (512-1024-1024-1024-1024-512-512-1321 neurons) with batch normalization, trained on a comprehensive dataset of 118,800 samples. The model achieves exceptional prediction accuracy with 99.45 % training and 99.33 % testing accuracy, significantly reducing computational costs compared to analytical methods. Comprehensive parametric analysis reveals critical insights into the effects of Mach number, CNT distribution patterns, honeycomb geometric parameters, and incident wave angles on STL performance. Key findings reveal that FG-X-CNTRC configuration achieves superior STL performance while the framework enables 750-fold computational speedup compared to analytical methods.
