AB  - Certain ocular diseases lead to irreparable vision impairment if not screened and treated early. Comorbities of ocular diseases is more common than assumed. This paper proposes a multiclass multilabel framework (MCMLF) based on fundus images for image-based, patient-based, and severity-based diagnosis without lesion segmentation or handcrafted feature extraction. The proposed system can detect the presence of any combination of ocular diseases that includes age-related macular degeneration (AMD), cataract, diabetic retinopathy (DR) and glaucoma, co-occurring in one or both eyes of a subject. The system involves a variational autoencoder (VAE) model to learn latent representations from fundus images, effectively addressing ambiguities caused by overlapping of spatial location and characteristics, thereby enabling the representation of multiple ocular diseases. Two-level data augmentation, including image augmentation and multilabel synthetic minority over-sampling technique (ML-SMOTE), addresses uneven sample distribution. Principal component analysis (PCA) fuses features from bilateral eyes for patient-based analysis, and a self-attention mechanism captures feature correlations. An ensemble sigmoid classifier chain model is used for classification. Evaluated on the ocular disease intelligent recognition (ODIR) and retinal fundus multi-disease image (RFMiD) databases, the system achieves area under the curve (AUC) values of 95.61 %, 94.06 % and 70.96 %, Kappa scores of 0.895, 0.898 and 0.806 and F1 scores of 93.2 %, 92.34 % and 85.19 % for image-based, patient-based and severity-based analysis, respectively. It is envisaged that the model will provide reliable assistance for large-scale screening and improve diagnostic efficiency in primary care settings.
