AB  - Phonocardiogram (PCG), electrocardiogram (ECG), and ballistocardiogram (BCG) are the non-stationary digital signals used in clinics for identifying various heart related disorders. Among these, PCG acquisition is more convenient. It is an acoustic signal obtained due to mechanical activities of heart valves and is used in clinics by various computer aided diagnosis (CAD) system for early diagnosis of cardiovascular diseases (CVDs), coronary heart diseases (CHD), status of heart, and its related structure etc. Early analysis helps facilitating initial intervention, which leads to the reduction in damage due to disease and improves long-term prognosis. PCG has an advantage over ECG because of its capacity to reflect the chronology of heart and the change force. This manuscript summarizes the various heart sounds correlated with heart diseases and the techniques used for identifying/classifying them along with their pros and cons by adhering to PRISMA-ScR guidelines. Approximately, 120-selected articles (sourced from Web-of-Science, Google Scholar, Publons, and Science Citation Index etc.,) through a systematic screening of titles, abstracts, and keywords related to heart disease detection and classification. These articles have been analyzed and discussed by grouping the techniques into traditional, Machine learning (ML), Deep learning (DL), and hybrid based approaches. These techniques are precisely summarized and tabulated for better understanding by the readers. Locally collected and publicly available various PCG databases used by the researchers are represented for future analysis. Performance metrics used by the researchers for evaluating the effectiveness of various methods for heart sound detection are represented. Based on the potential strength of various methods, a multi-modal (i.e., Synchronized analysis of PCG, ECG, And lung sound) artificial intelligence (AI) based analysis technique has been suggested for identifying the severity of various heart diseases. This approach uses preprocessing, Feature extraction (FE), Feature selection (FS), Classification by ML/DL/ensemble approaches to identify the severity of heart diseases from multi-modal signals. This study can serve as a reference point for automatic analysis of heart diseases from PCG. It can help the researchers to understand the limitations in PCG analysis techniques for identifying heart diseases for future research directives. Automatic analysis of PCG, May assist medical professionals with secondary early diagnosis forming non-invasive technique with lesser cost, Which eliminate cumbersome tests along with its correlation among other signals like ECG, Ballistocardiogram (BCG) and lung sounds etc.
