AB  - This study investigates the influence of velocity slip on the magnetohydrodynamic flow of a non-Newtonian Casson fluid between non-parallel walls. The governing nonlinear partial differential equations are reduced to a relatively simple nonlinear ordinary differential equation by applying a suitable similarity transformation. High-fidelity reference solutions generated using a boundary value problem solver are utilized to train a feedforward artificial neural network optimized via the Levenberg–Marquardt backpropagation algorithm. The Levenberg–Marquardt backpropagation algorithm demonstrated exceptional accuracy in predicting fluid dynamics, achieving low mean squared error values and strong correlations between predicted and target outcomes. The expected solutions of the suggested model are addressed by using the Levenberg–Marquardt adaptive neural network’s testing, training, and validation phases. Regression analysis, error histograms, function fitting graphs, and mean squared error outcomes are used to assess the algorithm’s performance and comparability. For each scenario in the case one, the performance on mean squared error for the velocity profile of the developed model was shown at around [2.4915×10−10,2.1404×10−9,9.8545×10−11,6.4551×10−10,2.2514×10−9]. A set of parameter values is used to compute the results: channel angle (α), Hartmann number (Ha), Reynolds number (Re), Casson fluid parameter (β), and velocity slip parameter (γ). In addition to detailed discussions, graphical simulation is used to illustrate the consequences of developing parameters for diverging and converging channels. The neural network model showed excellent predictive accuracy with the correlation coefficient (R)≈1. Key findings revealed that increasing the channel angle (α), Casson fluid parameter (β), velocity slip parameter (γ), and Hartmann number (Ha) enhanced fluid velocity, while Reynolds number (Re) exhibited opposing effects in diverging and converging channels. These results demonstrate the model’s robustness and the significant impact of physical parameters on Casson fluid dynamics. Accurate results are shown when the Levenberg–Marquardt method and reference solutions are compared.
