KW  - Variational autoencoders
AB  - In wireless communication, recognition of modulation method in a received signal is performed as an intermediate step between signal detection and demodulation. This functionality enhances the performance of wireless communication system through efficient demodulation and spectrum utilization. Most of the high-performance automatic modulation classification (AMC) algorithms employ deep neural networks due to their ability to learn intricate signal characteristics effectively. This paper aims to provide a comprehensive and critical review of deep learning algorithms for AMC in single-carrier and multi-carrier systems, including both single-antenna and multi-antenna scenarios. We present the fundamental concepts and analyze the effectiveness of various deep neural networks, including convolutional neural networks (CNN), recurrent architectures such as gated recurrent units (GRU) and long short-term memory (LSTM), hybrid models, autoencoders, and generative approaches such as generative adversarial networks (GAN) and variational autoencoders (VAE). In addition to the critical literature review, experimental evaluations for AMC are performed with RadioML2016.10a dataset using representative models, including CNN, GRU, CNN–LSTM, and GAN augmented CNN. The implemented algorithms are compared in terms of classification accuracy, number of parameters and total training time. Further, current research challenges and potential directions for future research in the field of AMC are also highlighted.
