AB  - Existing domain generalization semantic segmentation (DGSS) methods based on vision foundation models (VFMs) have achieved remarkable performance in unseen domains. Since VFMs are pre-trained on large-scale data from multiple domains, these methods freeze the backbone of VFMs to obtain rich representations and use them to train a cross-domain robust decoder for semantic segmentation. However, partial representations of VFMs, e.g., DINOv2, are shared across several domains, while others are effective only for several specific domains, which motivates us to enhance the domain-invariant information and eliminate the domain-specific information for representations of VFMs. In this paper, a prototype-guided domain-invariant enhancement and domain-specific normalization (PIESN) approach is proposed for DGSS. PIESN maintains two non-parametric matrices, including an intra-class relations invariance matrix and an inter-class relations difference matrix. The former is utilized to generate common representation embeddings for individual semantic classes, which aims to enhance domain-invariant information for each semantic class. The latter aims to normalize domain-specific information by keeping the consistent inter-class relations. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach has fewer trainable parameters and achieves superior performance on multiple benchmarks, e.g., achieving 66.8% average mIoU in the “Grand Theft Auto V (GTA5)→ {Cityscapes, Berkeley DeepDrive 100K (BDD100K), Mapillary Vistas Dataset (Mapillary)}” setting with 3.29 million (M) trainable parameters. Code is available at https://github.com/seabearlmx/PIESN.
