AB  - Knee injuries are among the leading causes of disability, impacting millions of individuals worldwide. The risk of developing knee osteoarthritis further underscores the importance of early detection in clinical settings. While deep learning has shown promise in knee injury classification, existing methods often suffer from limitations such as reliance on single Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) views, limited ability to simultaneously assess multiple types of tissue damage, and suboptimal integration of complementary imaging modalities. This paper introduces a novel Multiview Multimodal Multitask NETwork (M3NET), a three-dimensional deep learning model designed to address these limitations by utilizing multiview MRI scans alongside two-dimensional (2D) X-ray imaging. M3NET analyzes meniscus and cartilage damage by learning features from three-dimensional (3D) MRI and X-ray data. A fine-tuned You Only Look Once (YOLO) version 8 model automatically detects the knee joint region in MRI scans. The novel Separate-Feature Late Fusion (SFLF) architecture combines features from both modalities for consistent classification. Evaluated on 1519 MRI scans from the Osteoarthritis Initiative database, M3NET achieves state-of-the-art performance, demonstrating an area under the receiver operating curve of 86.9% for cartilage and 87.4% for meniscus damage detection. This simultaneous, high-performance, multi-modal assessment represents a significant advancement over existing single-view or single-modality approaches.
