AB  - Cracks are one of the major factors threatening the safety of dams. Underwater dam cracks are highly concealed, while both manual methods and traditional automatic image detection suffer from such problems as slow detection speeds, high false detection rates and poor robustness. Therefore, a deep learning model based on machine vision is proposed to achieve rapid real-time detection of underwater dam cracks. The improved model consists of a backbone, an encoder-decoder and a prediction head. Firstly, a novel Bheat module is used to reconstruct the backbone, enhancing visual representation capability for multi-scale features. Secondly, in the encoder, an efficient additive attention mechanism is added to strengthen intra-scale interactions of features and a lightweight upsampling operator, Dysample, is introduced to optimize the information fusion block, better aggregating multi-scale features and balancing the accuracy and efficiency. Finally, the Focaler-IoU loss function is utilized in the prediction head to solve the sample imbalance issue, enhancing the accuracy of crack detection. The above improvements not only enhance the model's adaptability and robustness in complex underwater environments, but also strike a balance between accuracy and efficiency for real-time crack detection. Additionally, the crack datasets captured by underwater drones is used to simulate various complex dam inspection scenarios, including motion blur, uneven lighting and obstacle interference, to evaluate the model's generalization ability. The ablation study demonstrates that the improved model achieves a good balance between accuracy and speed, with excellent performance of 0.857 mAP and 32.4 FPS.
