AB  - Intelligent Connected Vehicles (ICVs) are faced with the challenges of high-dimensional space complexity and dynamic coupling between individual decision-making and collective travel preferences in large-scale urban path planning. Traditional methods struggle to balance computational efficiency and adaptive optimization in spatio-temporal traffic evolution, particularly lacking capabilities to model the co-evolution of group preferences and network load. This paper proposes the Small-World Topology and Graph Attention Reinforcement Learning Strategy (SW-GRL). First, we construct a quantifiable small-world model to compress road networks into low-dimensional feature spaces while preserving congestion patterns. Second, we design a dueling deep Q-network enhanced by multi-head graph attention networks (GAT) to capture spatio-temporal traffic correlations, coupled with a multi-reward mechanism incorporating dynamic emission factors and travel time entropy constraints for joint optimization of efficiency, emissions, and user satisfaction. Finally, we develop a multi-scale feature fusion strategy for joint modeling of geospatial interpolation and topology, establishing dynamic weight associations between node-level and region-level features. SUMO simulation results on real road network topology show that compared with the baseline algorithm, SW-GRL reduces the average delay by 41.2%, reduces the energy consumption by 22.4%, and maintains the path optimality by 94.3%. By leveraging small-world structural properties and attention-driven policy learning, SW-GRL effectively resolves the NP-hard coupling optimization of group routing and network load balancing, establishing a novel topology-dynamic co-evolution paradigm for smart city traffic management.
