KW  - Geological context weight
AB  - Total organic carbon (TOC) content is a crucial information when assessing risk in unconventional oil and gas resources. Although conventional laboratory techniques for estimating TOC content provide high accuracy, they are usually expensive and inefficient for large datasets or routine analysis, primarily due to the time-consuming core sampling and complex chemical analysis processes. Currently, people have noticed the necessity of machine learning method to bypass these issues. But few people pay attention to Spatio-Temporal-Graph-Convolutional-Network (STGCN) method. This paper proposes a model that takes into account geological context weight——Geologically-Weighted-Spatial-Temporal-Graph-Convolutional-Network (GW-STGCN) model. The model decomposes the TOC prediction weight into feature background weight and geological context weight to reduce the impact of zero inflation problem, which refers to the sparse distribution issue of formation data leading to model overfitting. Additionally, lithology and rock density factors are introduced into the calculation of geological context weight. The transmission values of TOC prediction are weighted to effectively enhance the spatial feature extraction ability of the model. When tested on data from the Lizhai Depression of Zhangye Basin, GW-STGCN model achieved superior performance with a coefficient of determination (R2) of 0.94, a root mean square error (RMSE) of 0.04 and a mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.07, outperforming other models. The proposed method provides a cost-effective and efficient tool for TOC prediction, with significant implications for real-time formation evaluation and sweet spot identification in engineering applications. Overall, the GW-STGCN model represents a powerful and practical deep-learning solution for characterizing shale TOC content.
