AB  - The medical imaging sector focuses on explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) to make artificial intelligence (AI) models’ decision-making processes clear and understandable to doctors. By showing how models examine and classify medical images, XAI improves trust, accountability, and diagnostic precision. This model is crucial for enhancing clinical adoption and enabling ethical, informed utilisation of AI in healthcare. Bladder cancer is the 4th most common cancer. Initial diagnosis and treatment are essential to lessen the death rate of bladder cancer. Still, present diagnosis models are vulnerable to pathologist variabilities, and histopathological prediction techniques are inadequate to cover each feature of muscle-invasive bladder cancer. Diagnosed patients are assigned to risk groups, primarily based on stage and grade. Still, it is well-known that accurate estimation of bladder cancer develops intra-and inter-observer variabilities and adjustable reproducibility amongst pathologists, possibly resulting in under- or over-treatment of the patients. The economic burden, avoidable patient suffering, and the additional strain on the health care system exemplify the significance of emerging novel tools to support diagnosticians. Deep learning (DL) might offer new opportunities for predicting and diagnosing disease outcomes. DL can adaptively remove relevant image features based on learning objectives for tasks such as bladder cancer. In this study, an Enhanced Bladder Cancer Detection Using a Fusion of Feature Engineering and Hierarchical Representation Learning Model (EBCDFFE-HRLM) technique is proposed. The main objective of the EBCDFFE-HRLM technique is to develop an efficient and accurate model for detecting bladder cancer using medical imaging. Initially, the enhanced adaptive non-local means (EANLM) filtering method is employed in the image pre-processing phase to remove noise and improve image quality. Furthermore, the ConvNeXt, RegNet X, and MaxViT techniques are combined for feature extraction. Moreover, the EBCDFFE-HRLM model employs a deep belief network (DBN) for bladder cancer classification. Finally, XAI applies the SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) technique to make the decisions of these complex models more understandable. The EBCDFFE-HRLM approach is tested on the Bladder cancer classification dataset. The comparison results for the EBCDFFE-HRLM approach showed a superior accuracy of 98.75 % compared to existing models in bladder cancer detection.
