AB  - This study introduces the Encoding of Physics and Spatial Information in Latent-conditioned Fourier neural Operator Networks (EPSILON), a physics-informed neural operator (PINO) framework for accurately predicting quadrotor crash-area distributions under actuator failure conditions and environmental disturbances. Existing crash prediction approaches typically employ simplified ballistic assumptions or data-driven networks that lack physical consistency, limiting their ability to generalise across different flight conditions and fault scenarios. To address these shortcomings, EPSILON integrates spectral convolutional learning within Fourier Neural Operator (FNO) networks with latent conditioning, positional encoding, and physics-informed loss functions that incorporate both weighted mean-squared error and mass-conservation constraints. This hybrid formulation enables the network to learn spatially coherent and physically consistent crash area distributions. A high-fidelity dataset comprising 625,000 crash trajectories across 2500 scenarios was generated in Simulink with variations in mass, altitude, forward velocity, wind conditions, and actuator failure modes. Results from 5-fold cross-validation and hold-out tests demonstrate that EPSILON outperforms baseline multilayer perceptron (MLP) and physics-informed neural network (PINN) models, achieving up to 80% lower root mean squared error (RMSE). These findings highlight EPSILON’s enhanced predictive accuracy, physical interpretability, and spatial generalisation for multirotor safety applications.
