AB  - Dictionary learning is a machine learning approach that produces sparse representations robust to noise, more efficient to store and process. Learned dictionaries are used to capture underlying structures and patterns for image recognition and classification tasks. Engineering applications using distributed data sources use federated learning with edge devices. Federated learning enables collaborative model training across multiple devices or locations while satisfying the requirements of preserving data privacy and security. Currently, federated dictionary learning (FDL) methods use computationally intensive dictionary learning methods at each client. Computing efficiency and energy footprints can be improved by avoiding iterative singular value decomposition operations in FDL. Nonlinear federated dictionary learning, as presented here, is novel in the FDL context. The proposed federated random vector functional link-based dictionary learning avoids computationally intensive operations. The proposed method is a lean nonlinear FDL method that generates a dictionary and builds a classifier using feed-forward random vector functional links. At each client, the method has direct input–output weight connections from sparse coefficients to the original features of local samples. Sparse coefficients and their nonlinear transformations are used to learn dictionary and classifier matrices. The proposed FedJoin method for model aggregation is faster than other model aggregation methods. The nonlinear FDL method has shown more accurate results as compared to existing nonlinear dictionary learning methods implemented in a federated manner. Empirical evidence of superiority in scalability and efficiency is shown with experimentation on industrial process monitoring datasets, optical character recognition datasets, and a face recognition dataset.
