AB  - Air pollution poses a significant challenge to modern society, with severe impacts on public health and national economies. To combat this, governments rely on air quality monitoring stations to measure pollutant concentrations and implement preventive measures. However, the high costs of installing and maintaining these stations lead to sparse spatial coverage, leaving policymakers and the public without adequate information to address air pollution risks effectively. Recent studies have attempted to estimate pollutant concentrations in areas without monitoring stations but face two primary limitations: (i) a focus on very recent time periods, neglecting long-term temporal dependencies, and (ii) reliance solely on spatial relationships between unknown locations and monitoring stations, overlooking critical network-wide information. To address these challenges, we propose the Dynamic Temporal Fusion Graph Neural Network (DTFGNN). This model incorporates a Long and Short Temporal Fusion module that captures short-term temporal correlations using Gated Recurrent Units and long-term dependencies with a Diagonal Structured State Space model. Additionally, we introduce a Dynamic Adjacency Constructor module that dynamically learns the dependencies across the entire network. Experiments on two real-world datasets demonstrate that DTFGNN outperforms existing approaches, achieving up to an 11% reduction in root mean square error.
