AB  - Heterogeneous graph neural networks (HGNNs) are powerful tools for heterogeneous graph mining, yet they face critical limitations when applied to cold-start patent analysis scenarios: existing HGNNs rely on predefined metapaths with poor cross-domain generalization, fail to align pre-training and downstream tasks, and perform poorly with limited labeled data; prompt-based graph methods, originally designed for homogeneous graphs, cannot capture the complex heterogeneous semantics inherent in patent data. To address these issues, this paper proposes Heterogeneous Patent Graph Prompt Learning (HPGPL), a graph framework that integrates heterogeneous graph neural networks with prompt learning to tackle cold-start challenges in patent classification and clustering. HPGPL constructs heterogeneous patent graphs by connecting application numbers, patentees, and claim keywords via bidirectional relationships, effectively linking diverse patents across technical domains. It learns transferable metapath semantics through self-supervised pre-training and adapts to downstream tasks using learnable prompt vectors, eliminating the need for metapath adjustment and full model retraining. Experiments on four real-world datasets show HPGPL outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, it achieves an average 19.34% gain in patent clustering, a 4.37% average gain in patent classification, and a maximum 111.53% improvement in Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) under cold-start conditions. This framework effectively overcomes label scarcity in patent analysis, enhancing generalization across technical domains and providing a practical solution for intellectual property knowledge discovery.
