AB  - Aspect-based sentiment analysis (ABSA) is a fine-grained sentiment analysis task that aims to identify the sentiment polarity associated with each aspect term in a sentence. In recent years, studies in ABSA have achieved promising results by integrating syntactic information with graph neural networks (GNNs). However, most existing methods that incorporate structural information from constituency trees rely on node paths as additional features combined with the original data for model training. These approaches often ignore phrase-level range information in constituency trees, which may result in incomplete or redundant syntactic feature extraction. To address this limitation, we propose a Dual Syntax Bidirectional Interactive Network (DSBIN). First, we introduce an Adaptive Optimal Level (AOL) algorithm to dynamically extract the most informative syntactic structures from constituency trees, and then employ graph attention networks (GATs) to model constituency-based syntactic features. Meanwhile, syntactic information from dependency trees is captured using another set of graph attention networks. Furthermore, a Bidirectional Interactive Attention (BIA) mechanism is designed to enable mutual interaction between the two types of syntactic representations, thereby achieving syntactic complementarity. Experimental results on four benchmark datasets demonstrate that the proposed DSBIN model achieves advanced performance, validating the effectiveness of the proposed approach. Compared with large language models (LLMs), the proposed DSBIN exhibits clear advantages in ABSA tasks.
